  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1154 AM MDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nLAST OF THE MOISTURE:\n\nThe shortwave that moved through yesterday has caused flow to become \nwesterly. That change in flow has allowed dry air to start to filter \ninto the area, which should result in less coverage and showers and \nstorms in the afternoon. Despite that we are expecting convection to \ndevelop in the mainly along and north of I-70 this afternoon and \nearly evening with more isolated coverage south of the interstate. \nShowers should form over the higher terrain and then move \neastward over the adjacent valleys. It appears there will be \nenough instability and moisture to support the potential for \nheavy rain, small hail and perhaps gusty winds. Tomorrow more \ndry air filters into the area to the point that only the Divide \na southern San Juans have the chance for showers and storms. \n\n\nHOT AND DRY:\n\nOver the next several days high pressure is expected to drift over \nthe desert southwest. This will bring warmer temperatures and a \ndrier airmass to the region. All of that results in record high \ntemperatures this weekend and into next week for many locations. \nAlso, localized critical fire weather is possible through that \nperiod. Winds speeds in the afternoon may reach 25 mph in spots with \nminimum humidity in the teens and single digits. Afternoon showers \nmay still be possible along the Divide, but elsewhere stays dry \nthrough mid next week. On Monday, a trough tracks over the northern \ntier of the country, which may suppress the high pressure slightly. \nBeyond that temperatures still look well above normal and the high \nshifts to our southeast. This may bring moisture back into the area \nthat could support afternoon storms once again.",
